WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.720
If you go to a Google map of Rio de Janeiro and put it into 3D mode,

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.580
you can see what the city looks like as it

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.139
was designed by urban planners.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.580
But you will also notice parts of the city
that don’t look like the rest.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.680
See the difference?

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.640
The people who live in these parts of the city,

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.980
with the clean lines and the well thought-out design,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.600
are called
“people of the asphalt.”

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.840
The people who live in these parts of the city are called
“people of the hill.”

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.730
Even though the people of the asphalt and
the people of the hill live closely intertwined

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.570
throughout the city, they live vastly different
lives.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.360
These informal communities that look like houses stacked on top of each other,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.840
sprouting out of the jungle,

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:45.980
are called Favelas,
home to both vicious drug gangs as well as some

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.670
of the most peaceful, creative, and resourceful
people in Rio.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.960
I want to show you want they looks like on
the inside.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.759
A favela is a community that was built without
any oversight from a public authority--no

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.880
zoning, no building codes, no public services.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.780
These places just grew out of the hills over
time thanks to two main factors:

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.300
First was slavery.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.780
Brazil imported 11 times more slaves than
the United States and Rio alone was home to

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.500
more slaves than the entire American south.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:33.080
Slavery ended in 1888, and free slaves, still denied
many rights in society, built informal communities

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:33.680
on their own.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.540
In more recent times, favelas have been fueled
by massive migrations,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.860
from rural Brazilians coming into the city looking for work.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.180
Not able to find affordable housing, these
workers built their own communities.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.280
Today, 25% of Rio’s residents live in these
favelas.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:56.750
I spent time in 6 of Rio’s favelas to figure
out what happens when parts of a city develop

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.460
without the presence of a government.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.100
This is Rocinha.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.410
It’s Brazil's largest favela and has been
dubbed a city within a city.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:12.100
It’s a completely self sufficient economy,
the result of decades of makeshift solutions

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.940
to basic needs like electricity and running
water.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:20.130
Without a formal government presence, the
residents of Rocinha created their own association

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.800
which helps coordinate public
projects and resources.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:29.250
Since these associations grew up totally informally
by people who had no training in public administration,

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:34.820
the resulting community design brings with it a little
more zest and creativity than your traditional city.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.400
But make no mistake, Rocinha is a full-on
functioning mini city with the city of Rio de Janeiro.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.220
This impromptu resourcefulness is common within
favelas.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.100
Here I am in Vidigal, a favela not far from
Rocinha.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.540
This man Paulo is showing me his garden.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.130
But it wasn’t always a garden.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.880
15 years ago, this hill that we stand on,

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.480
was teeming with garbage.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:13.190
Paulo decided to cleaned it up, planted trees and cultivated
a garden space that now produces fruit.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.800
He did this without asking permission,

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.540
because, after all, there was no one to ask permission from.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.340
If you look around the graden, you'll realize that everything is made from trash.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.700
On the other side of the city is a Favela
called Maré.

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:38.520
The people of this community have created
art centers for young people to come learn

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:39.520
new skills.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.960
They also support established artists to create
projects around the city that explore

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.700
and communicate life in the favela.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.060
Here they are building a model of their favela
out of recycled wood.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.820
In Providencia, a favela near the port zone,
I met up with this guy.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.860
Mauricio, a photographer who lives in this
amazing house.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.520
Mauricio photographs life in the favela, providing
transparency to the good and the bad of these places

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.600
He thinks of photography as a weapon to fight
against everything from drug cartels to the

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.120
government when they show up trying to remove
parts of his community

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.060
Whenever he sees corruption or foul play in his community,

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.690
he photographs it and distributes it to a network of local

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:25.480
and international media contacts.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.860
Over the years, people have learned not to mess with him.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.880
Why are these people who live in poverty and
neglect,

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.920
so driven to create beauty in order to survive?

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.740
This reality of neglect from public investment,

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.320
has created a culture of creative survival.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.020
But there’s a dark to this too.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:14.320
Right now we are traveling over Complexo Do Alemão,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.880
which is a huge complex or block of favelas

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.140
We're not going into the streets today, because this place is still very much run by

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:25.140
drug trafficking gangs

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.540
I would be sugar coating the situation if I didn't talk about the fact that drug gangs

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:35.900
stil have major influence in a lot of the favelas and Alemão is one of those places.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:41.520
Perhaps the most powerful gang in Rio is called the Red Command, a group that began as a left-wing political

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:44.570
rebellion and whose headquarters are in Alemão.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:50.640
Cocaine arrived in Rio in the 1980s, enriching
the gangs and allowing them to grow in power and territory.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:55.980
The Red Command became more violent and lost
its political ideology, focusing entirely

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.760
on drug and arms trafficking.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.160
The fact that favelas aren't formal and aren't regulated, means that

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.120
both that they can become incredibly vibrant

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:11.040
because people can take this attitude and build on qualities and be creative and change your environment,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.220
but it also means that you can get incredibly dysfunctional places

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.120
when the energy and the approach is the opposite.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.580
So you have these two extremes and they come out of the same force:

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.480
informality, lack of regulation, and flexibility.

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:30.700
In 2008, the city of Rio was ready to take
over this lawless territories of Rio.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:36.840
They assembled a special force of police officers
to enter the favelas and drive out the gang influence.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.140
They call this process “pacification of
the favelas”

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:41.840
But this gets tricky really fast.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:46.120
There’s been a big discussion in the United States
about police brutality.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:50.800
But Brazil is on a whole different level when
it comes to police violence and corruption.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:57.080
Human right watch estimates US police officers
kill one person in every 37,000 arrests.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:01.440
In Rio that number is 1 in every 23 arrests.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:07.120
So you can see why some felt skeptical of letting the police come into the favelas to try to restore order.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:11.000
This is Santa Marta.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.360
It’s the first favela that received pacification
forces back in 2008.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.420
It also happens to be the place where Michael Jackson decided to shoot a music video.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:47.700
Pacification worked for Santa Marta and a few other favelas,

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:49.520
for the first few years after 2008,

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.800
but this favela is small, and the city dedicated
its best police forces to the job.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:58.770
It’s been a whole different experience in a place
like Alemão and other bigger favelas.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:02.580
Many of the favelas that I visited that had apparently been pacified,

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.960
were still very clearly under the influence of the Red Command.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.460
So while there have been some successes in pacification,

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.950
the city still has a huge challenge ahead of it

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:14.830
in taking control of these places.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:20.440
International attention paid to favelas is
usually directed towards the conflict between

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:22.210
the gangs and police.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:24.560
There’s movies and video games about this.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.660
This problem has been perhaps disproportionately amplified across the world.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:35.880
But while gang violence is certainly a problem,
it represents one small slice of the favela experience.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.380
What seems to me as the more striking and interesting aspect of favelas,

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:42.979
are the thousands of men and women who are thriving

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:46.260
in creative way in spite of being neglected
by their government.

